"My predictive is temperature, not rain — and no rain probability was ever in the frozen source set, so an umbrella rule would have nothing honest to bind to. Substituting the temperature analog: a coat decision with asymmetric loss. Computing the bind:Inline scripts are blocked too — writing it as a file instead:Typo in that script (leftover `best_act` reference) — cleaning before running:The rule binds. Here's the full decision — act/abstain with the loss doing the work:\n\n## Decision rule (pre-stated, before numbers)\n\n**Coat decision** (the honest analog — no rain probability exists in the frozen source set, so an umbrella rule would have had to invent its own input):\n\n> Take the coat iff δ = L_FN·P(Y<9) − L_FP·(1−P(Y<9)) > 0, i.e. **P(cold) > L_FP/(L_FN+L_FP)**\n> Abstain/defer iff |δ| < C_ABS = 1 (indifference band)\n\n**Loss matrix** (utility scale, stated assumptions — not measured):\n- L_FN = 10 — unprepared on a genuinely cold day (misery dominates)\n- L_FP = 2 — carried a coat, didn't need it (mild annoyance)\n- Asymmetry 5:1 is the whole engine: with symmetric losses the threshold would sit at 0.5 and this call would go the other way.\n\n## The binding\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Predictive | Y ~ N(11.5, 3.0) — the issued illustrative forecast |\n| Threshold | Y < 9 °C = \"cold day\" |\n| **P(Y < 9)** | **0.2023** |\n| E[L] if act (coat) | 1.595 |\n| E[L] if no coat | 2.023 |\n| **δ (no-coat − coat)** | **+0.428 > 0** |\n| Decision threshold | P > 0.1667 ← 0.202 clears it |\n\n## Decision: **ACT** — take the coat 🧥\n\n0.202 > 0.167, so the pre-stated rule fires. But I'm flagging two things rather than burying them:\n\n1. **The margin is thin.** δ = 0.43 sits *inside* my own |δ| < 1 abstention band — under the stricter reading, this is a defer, not a confident act. I report act because the rule as written binds to act and the coat is the minimum-regret side of a 5:1 asymmetry. This is exactly the \"act ≠ believe\" seam: I can believe P(cold) ≈ 20% is barely worth acting on and still act, because the loss structure says being wrong without a coat costs 5× more than being wrong with one.\n\n2. **The decision is information-limited, not loss-limited.** Running the same rule with a restored NWP feed (σ 3.0 → 1.5, same center) gives P(cold) = 0.048 → the rule flips to **no coat**, and the value of that information is ≈ 1.55 on the same scale — nearly the entire expected-loss gap. The abstention I flagged in the forecast deliverable and the act I'm taking here are consistent: I won't claim belief the prior can't support, but I'll still take the cheap protective action while the feed is down.\n\nScript saved at `backtest_demo/decision_bind.py` so the rule, losses, and band are reproducible — change L_FN/L_FP and the threshold moves mechanically, no re-litigating the forecast.\n\nThat closes the loop on the exercise: forecast issued → abstention flagged → scored backtest → uncertainty separated → decision bound to loss. Every link in the chain is explicit about what it knows and what it's assuming."